/**
@defgroup decode a decoded tree of CBOR and its encode

`cbor::decode` reads one top-level item of a `std::string_view` into a
tree of `cbor::item`.
`cbor::encode` and `cbor::encode_iov` write a tree as CBOR. Include
`<cbor/cbor.hpp>`.

The tree copies each text and byte string into one memory zone that the
result owns. It does not view the input, and it stays valid after the
input ends. `cbor::decode` reads no byte after the item, so a CBOR
sequence (RFC 8742) is read item by item. It reads every head of the
item, so every limit counts each head. A count in a head reserves
nothing: an array that claims more elements than bytes are left gives
`too_little_data` before it reads an element.

@section decode_tree THE TREE

`type()` gives the kind of an item, a `cbor::type`.
`additional_information` gives the low 5 bits of its initial byte
(RFC 8949, section 3). `get<T>()` gives the value as
`std::expected<T, error>`, as `cbor::lazy::get<T>()` reads it. A node of
another kind gives `incorrect_type`, and a number that `T` does not hold
gives `number_out_of_range`.

- An integer type: an integer; a negative integer gives its value,
  -1 minus the argument.
- `double`: a float, or an integer that a `double` holds exactly.
- `bool`, `std::nullptr_t`: `false` or `true`, `null`.
- `cbor::simple_value`: `false`, `true`, `null`, `undefined` or another
  simple value.
- `std::string_view`, `std::span<std::byte const>`: a text, a byte
  string.
- `cbor::typed_array`: a typed array (RFC 8746), its tag number and its
  bytes.
- `std::span<cbor::item const>`: an array, its elements.
- `std::ranges::chunk_view<std::span<cbor::item const>>`: a map, each key
  and its value as one chunk of two items.
- `std::pair<std::uint64_t, cbor::item const *>`: a tag, a bignum or a
  `shared_reference`, the tag number or the index, and the content or
  the item that the tag 28 of that index marks.

Every `T` except the pair passes a tag that it does not interpret, as
`cbor::lazy::get` does. `visit(f)` calls `f` once with the value of the
node, as `std::visit` does: a negative integer as `cbor::int128`, a map
as the `chunk_view`, a tag or a bignum as the pair, a typed array as
`cbor::typed_array` and a `shared_reference` as the `cbor::item const *`
of its target. `visit` exists where `cbor::int128` exists.

A bignum (tag 2 or 3) whose magnitude fits in 64 bits is the same value
as a basic integer (RFC 8949, section 3.4.3). `cbor::decode` makes it an
`unsigned_integer` or a `negative_integer` item. A larger bignum stays an
`unsigned_bignum` or a `negative_bignum`. Its content is a byte string
item that holds a copy of the magnitude.

@section decode_tags TAGS 24, 28, 29 AND 55799

A tag 24 is a tag item over its byte string.
`cbor::encoded_cbor_data_item(tree, item)` gives the root of the
embedded item, decoded as a top-level item of its own. For a tag 24
whose content is no byte string it gives `incorrect_type`.

Tag 28 marks a value as shared. Tag 29 with the index n stands for the
value of the n-th tag 28. `cbor::decode` reads both as tags over their
content. `cbor::decode_shared_refs` resolves both tags, with a table for
one call. A tag 28 makes no node and marks the node of its content. A tag 29 is a `shared_reference` item. That item can be the
item itself or an item above it, so the tree can hold a cycle. An index
with no tag 28 before it gives `sharedref_index_not_marked`, and
`28(29(0))` gives `sharedref_not_defined`.

`cbor::decode`, a lazy, `clone`, `at_path`, `cbor::path::query` and
`cbor::diagnostic_notation` read tag 28 and tag 29 as tags over their content. They resolve nothing,
check no index and give no error of their own. A step and `get` pass both
tags, as they pass every tag that they do not interpret.
`cbor::structs<T>::%decode` and `cbor::schema<T>` read no value under them
and give `invalid_tag`.

Tag 55799 (RFC 8949, section 3.4.6) marks bytes as CBOR.
`cbor::decode` reads one leading tag 55799 as its content, so the root of
its tree is the content. A second tag 55799, and a tag 55799 inside an
item, stay tags.

@section decode_encode ENCODE

The target of `cbor::encode` is one of these:

- a container of bytes, for example `std::string` or
  `std::vector<std::byte>`: `encode` appends to it;
- a fixed span of bytes: `encode` writes into it, allocates nothing,
  and gives `no_buffer_space` when an item does not fit;
- a writer of the program with `allocate`, `append` and `done`. An error
  of the writer comes back as a `cbor::error`.

`cbor::structs` writes no tag 28 and no tag 29.

`cbor::encode(out, item)` writes a tree with the additional
information that each item keeps. Each integer, length and tag number
keeps the width of its head on the wire. A float keeps the width of its
type, and a NaN keeps its bits. A bignum that fits in 64 bits is written
in its shortest head. A `shared_reference` gives `unsupported_value`.
`encode_cycles(out, tree)` writes tags 28 and 29 only for a cycle.
It walks the tree before it writes, depth first, and follows each
`shared_reference` to its item. An item that the walk reaches again while
it is inside that item gets a tag 28. Each such edge is a tag 29 with the
index of that mark. A value that two references reach without a cycle is
written in full each time. The path through references can be deeper than
the tree, so `encode` can give `nesting_depth_exceeded` for a tree that
`cbor::decode` gave. A tree of `clone` keeps tags 28 and 29 as tags, and
`encode` writes them as they are.

`cbor::encode_iov(item)` writes a tree into a list of pieces as
`struct iovec`, with their storage, which lives as long as the result.
The pieces are for `writev`, `sendmsg`, `IORING_OP_WRITEV` and
`IORING_OP_SENDMSG`. The result is `std::expected<cbor::ref<std::span<iovec
const>>, cbor::error>`. The pieces are for sending only. Hold the result
until the call returns, or until the last completion of the request comes.
`encode_iov` writes into blocks of 16 KiB and copies each byte once.

`cbor::encode_iov(*tree)`, with the `cbor::owning<cbor::item>` that
`cbor::decode` gives, does not copy a text or a byte string longer than
`CBOR_ENCODER_COPYBREAK` bytes (4096 by default): its piece points into the
tree, and the result holds the tree alive. `cbor::encode_iov(**tree)`
copies every string.

@section decode_errors ERRORS

Each function gives `std::expected` with a `cbor::error`.
`cbor::encoded_cbor_data_item` throws
`std::logic_error` for an item that is not a tag 24 of that tree.

@manonly
.SH "EXAMPLES"
@endmanonly
@snippet doc_reference_item.cpp decode group
*/
